// rosexec.nv — the executor, as a step a caller drives rather than a
// loop that calls back.
//
// Every body here is a `todo()`; the signatures are the design.
//
// `[]` THROUGHOUT and `@tier(embedded)` on all of it.  The executor
// here decides WHAT to run; the caller runs it.
//
// THE INVERSION IS THE LOAD-BEARING DECISION.  rclc's executor takes a
// function pointer per handle and calls it from inside `spin_some`.
// Written that way in novo-lang, every registered callback would have
// to share ONE effect row, because they are all stored in one table of
// the same function type — so a node with a subscription callback that
// writes a GPIO and another that only updates a counter would charge
// both, and every caller of `spin_some`, with hardware access.  A
// `@value` struct cannot appear in a function value's signature either
// (E2015), so the message could not be one.
//
// So `spin_step` answers WHICH handle is ready and the caller
// dispatches it with a `match`.  Three things come out of that beyond
// the effect rows: the dispatch is ordinary code a reader can follow,
// a test can drive the whole executor with no middleware under it, and
// a callback can borrow whatever it likes from the enclosing scope
// because it is not a closure stored in a table.
//
// THE ORDER HANDLES WERE ADDED IS THE SCHEDULE.  rclc dispatches ready
// handles in registration order and nothing in its API says so, so a
// node whose control callback runs before its sensor callback has a
// one-cycle delay in its loop that moving two lines of setup code
// removes.  `add_handle` answers the index it assigned, and
// `handle_order` is that fact made readable.
//
// PUBLISHERS ARE NOT HANDLES.  An executor waits on things that become
// READY — subscriptions, timers, services, clients and guard conditions
// — and a publisher never does.  Counting publishers when sizing the
// executor wastes slots; forgetting timers exhausts it.
// `handles_required` is the arithmetic, because getting it wrong is a
// runtime failure on a device.

// ── How many handles ─────────────────────────────────────────────────

// The most handles one executor here can hold: 64.
//
// The ready set is a bit per handle in one integer, which is what lets
// a spin be a value rather than a list.  Sixty-four handles is far more
// than a microcontroller node has; a node that wants more wants two
// executors, which is also how rclc gets priorities.
pub const ROS_EXEC_MAX_HANDLES = 64

// How many executor handles a node needs.
//
// Subscriptions, timers, services, clients and guard conditions, added
// up.  PUBLISHERS ARE NOT COUNTED: nothing waits on one.
//
// ```novo
// use rosexec
//
// fn main() [io]
//     // Two subscriptions, one timer, no services, no clients, no
//     // guard conditions — and three publishers, which cost nothing.
//     println("${rosexec.handles_required(2, 1, 0, 0, 0)}")   // 3
// ```
pub @tier(embedded)
fn handles_required(subscriptions: Int, timers: Int, services: Int, clients: Int,
                    guards: Int) -> Int []
    todo("ros2-nv.rosexec.handles_required")

// ── When a spin dispatches ───────────────────────────────────────────

// Dispatch as soon as any handle is ready.  rclc's default, and what a
// node that just wants to react uses.
pub const ROS_TRIGGER_ANY = 0
// Dispatch only when EVERY handle is ready.
//
// What makes a fusion node deterministic: a filter that needs a reading
// from three sensors runs once per complete set rather than three times
// per set with two thirds of its input stale.  The cost is that one
// silent sensor stops the node.
pub const ROS_TRIGGER_ALL = 1
// Dispatch when one named handle is ready, whatever the others are
// doing.  A timer-driven node whose subscriptions only fill buffers.
pub const ROS_TRIGGER_ONE = 2

// ── What the two semantics mean ──────────────────────────────────────

// Each callback sees whatever has arrived by the time it runs.
//
// rclcpp's behaviour, and rclc's default.  A message published by one
// callback can be taken by a later callback in the SAME spin, so a
// three-stage chain propagates in one cycle — and how long that cycle
// takes depends on which callbacks happened to be ready.
pub const ROS_SEMANTICS_ROS2 = 0

// Every input is taken at the START of the spin and every output is
// published at the END.
//
// Logical execution time.  A three-stage chain now takes three spins,
// which is slower and is the point: the delay through the node is the
// same on every cycle whatever the timing was, so a controller tuned on
// a bench behaves the same under load.  A callback cannot see data a
// callback earlier in the same spin produced, which is the property
// that makes the timing independent of the order.
pub const ROS_SEMANTICS_LET = 1

// ── When a callback runs ─────────────────────────────────────────────

// Run the callback only when a new message arrived.
pub const ROS_INVOKE_ON_NEW_DATA = 0

// Run the callback every spin, with or without a message.
//
// How a control loop keeps its period when its input is late.  THE
// CALLBACK GETS NO MESSAGE on the spins where nothing arrived, and a
// callback written for the other setting reads a buffer holding either
// nothing or the previous message — which is why `RosExecStep` carries
// `has_message` as a field rather than leaving it to be inferred.
pub const ROS_INVOKE_ALWAYS = 1

// ── The executor ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// A wait set and a dispatch policy, with no handles' contents in it.
//
// What a handle IS — which subscription, which callback — lives with
// the program.  What lives here is the index, the kind and the two
// policies, which is everything the dispatch decision needs.
pub @value
struct RosExecutor
    // How many handles this executor was built for.
    capacity: Int
    // How many have been added.
    count: Int
    // One of `ROS_TRIGGER_*`.
    trigger: Int
    // The handle `ROS_TRIGGER_ONE` waits for, or -1.
    trigger_index: Int
    // One of `ROS_SEMANTICS_*`.
    semantics: Int
    // How long a spin should wait for something to become ready, in
    // milliseconds.  A number this package never sleeps for: the wait
    // is the transport's, and this is what the caller passes it.
    timeout_ms: Int
    // Which handle the next `spin_step` will consider first.  A spin
    // walks the handles in registration order and this is where it is.
    cursor: Int

// An executor with room for `capacity` handles.
//
// Trigger any, ROS 2 semantics, a hundred millisecond spin timeout —
// rclc's own defaults.
//
// ```novo
// use rosexec
//
// fn main() [io]
//     println("${rosexec.executor(3).capacity}")   // 3
// ```
pub @tier(embedded)
fn executor(capacity: Int) -> RosExecutor []
    todo("ros2-nv.rosexec.executor")

// How many more handles will fit.
pub @tier(embedded)
fn remaining_handles(e: RosExecutor) -> Int []
    todo("ros2-nv.rosexec.remaining_handles")

// Whether one more handle fits.
pub @tier(embedded)
fn can_add_handle(e: RosExecutor) -> Bool []
    todo("ros2-nv.rosexec.can_add_handle")

// The executor with one more handle.
//
// UNCHANGED when it is full: `add_handle_ret` is the refusal, and a
// caller that ignored it would have an executor whose count disagrees
// with what it thinks it registered.
pub @tier(embedded)
fn add_handle(e: RosExecutor) -> RosExecutor []
    todo("ros2-nv.rosexec.add_handle")

// The return code adding a handle would produce.
//
// `ROS_RET_OK`, or `ROS_RET_WAIT_SET_FULL` — which is the code that
// says the number passed to `executor` was smaller than the number
// `handles_required` answers.
pub @tier(embedded)
fn add_handle_ret(e: RosExecutor) -> Int []
    todo("ros2-nv.rosexec.add_handle_ret")

// Which position the next handle added will take.
//
// THE POSITION IS THE SCHEDULE.  Ready handles are dispatched in this
// order on every spin, so a node whose control callback was registered
// before the subscription it consumes runs one cycle behind for the
// life of the program — and neither the code nor the middleware says
// so anywhere.
pub @tier(embedded)
fn handle_order(e: RosExecutor) -> Int []
    todo("ros2-nv.rosexec.handle_order")

// The executor with a different trigger policy.
//
// `index` is the handle `ROS_TRIGGER_ONE` waits on, and is ignored by
// the other two.
pub @tier(embedded)
fn set_trigger(e: RosExecutor, trigger: Int, index: Int) -> RosExecutor []
    todo("ros2-nv.rosexec.set_trigger")

// The executor with different semantics.
pub @tier(embedded)
fn set_semantics(e: RosExecutor, semantics: Int) -> RosExecutor []
    todo("ros2-nv.rosexec.set_semantics")

// The executor with a different spin timeout, in milliseconds.
pub @tier(embedded)
fn set_timeout(e: RosExecutor, timeout_ms: Int) -> RosExecutor []
    todo("ros2-nv.rosexec.set_timeout")

// The semantics in words.
pub @tier(embedded)
fn semantics_name(semantics: Int) -> Str []
    todo("ros2-nv.rosexec.semantics_name")

// The trigger policy in words.
pub @tier(embedded)
fn trigger_name(trigger: Int) -> Str []
    todo("ros2-nv.rosexec.trigger_name")

// ── A spin ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// Whether a ready set satisfies the executor's trigger policy.
//
// `ready` is a bit per handle, bit `n` for the handle registered `n`th.
// A bit set means the middleware says that handle has something.
//
// ```novo
// use rosexec
//
// fn main() [io]
//     let e = rosexec.set_trigger(rosexec.executor(3), rosexec.ROS_TRIGGER_ALL, -1)
//     println("${rosexec.should_dispatch(e, 0x3)}")   // false, two of three
//     println("${rosexec.should_dispatch(e, 0x7)}")   // true
// ```
pub @tier(embedded)
fn should_dispatch(e: RosExecutor, ready: Int) -> Bool []
    todo("ros2-nv.rosexec.should_dispatch")

// The bit in a ready set that belongs to handle `index`.
pub @tier(embedded)
fn ready_bit(index: Int) -> Int []
    todo("ros2-nv.rosexec.ready_bit")

// Whether handle `index` is ready in this set.
pub @tier(embedded)
fn is_ready(ready: Int, index: Int) -> Bool []
    todo("ros2-nv.rosexec.is_ready")

// How many handles are ready.
pub @tier(embedded)
fn ready_count(ready: Int) -> Int []
    todo("ros2-nv.rosexec.ready_count")

// What one step of a spin says to do.
pub @value
struct RosExecStep
    // Whether there is anything to dispatch.  False ends the spin.
    dispatch: Bool
    // Which handle, in registration order.
    index: Int
    // One of `rosgraph`'s `ROS_ENTITY_*` values, as the caller
    // registered it.
    kind: Int
    // Whether a message came with it.  FALSE under
    // `ROS_INVOKE_ALWAYS` on a spin where nothing arrived, and a
    // callback that reads a buffer without checking this reads either
    // nothing or the previous message.
    has_message: Bool
    // `ROS_RET_OK`, or the code the take produced.  Very often
    // `ROS_RET_SUBSCRIPTION_TAKE_FAILED`, which is NOT an error — see
    // `rosret`.
    ret: Int

// What to do next, given what the middleware says is ready.
//
// Answers a step with `dispatch` false when the trigger policy is not
// satisfied and when the walk has passed the last handle.  A spin is
// this called in a loop until it answers false, with the executor it
// returns from `spin_advance` each time.
//
// THE CALLER DISPATCHES.  That is the whole shape of this module: the
// step names a handle and the caller's own `match` decides what running
// it means, so the effects a callback has are the caller's and not
// every caller's.
pub @tier(embedded)
fn spin_step(e: RosExecutor, ready: Int) -> RosExecStep []
    todo("ros2-nv.rosexec.spin_step")

// The executor after a step has been dispatched.
pub @tier(embedded)
fn spin_advance(e: RosExecutor) -> RosExecutor []
    todo("ros2-nv.rosexec.spin_advance")

// The executor at the start of a new spin.
//
// Puts the cursor back to the first handle.  Called once per spin, and
// the reason the cursor is a field rather than a loop variable: a node
// that has to give the scheduler its turn can stop a spin halfway and
// resume it.
pub @tier(embedded)
fn spin_begin(e: RosExecutor) -> RosExecutor []
    todo("ros2-nv.rosexec.spin_begin")

// Whether a spin has walked every handle.
pub @tier(embedded)
fn spin_done(e: RosExecutor) -> Bool []
    todo("ros2-nv.rosexec.spin_done")

// Which handles a spin under these semantics may take from.
//
// Under ROS 2 semantics, everything in the ready set.  Under logical
// execution time, the ready set AS IT WAS when the spin began, which is
// why this takes both: a handle that became ready during the spin is
// dispatched on the next one, and that is the property that makes the
// delay through the node constant.
pub @tier(embedded)
fn takeable(e: RosExecutor, ready_at_begin: Int, ready_now: Int) -> Int []
    todo("ros2-nv.rosexec.takeable")

// ── Timers ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// A late timer fires as many times as it missed, catching up.
//
// rcl's own behaviour.  The consequence is worth stating: a control
// loop whose spin was blocked for a second runs its integrator ten
// times in a row with the same interval, and a PID controller does
// something dramatic.
pub const ROS_TIMER_CATCH_UP = 0

// A late timer fires once and the missed periods are dropped.
//
// What a control loop wants, and what a counter of events does not.
pub const ROS_TIMER_SKIP_MISSED = 1

// A periodic callback, counted in the caller's own milliseconds.
//
// NO CLOCK.  This package reads no time: `advance` takes how many
// milliseconds passed, from whatever the program uses to measure them.
// That also makes a node's timing replayable from a log, and it is what
// lets the same timer run against simulated time without a second
// implementation.
pub @value
struct RosTimer
    // How often it should fire, in milliseconds.
    period_ms: Int
    // How long since it last fired.
    elapsed_ms: Int
    // One of `ROS_TIMER_*`.
    on_late: Int
    // How many firings are owed, under `ROS_TIMER_CATCH_UP`.
    owed: Int
    // Whether the timer runs at all.
    active: Bool

// A timer of this period.
pub @tier(embedded)
fn timer(period_ms: Int, on_late: Int) -> RosTimer []
    todo("ros2-nv.rosexec.timer")

// The timer after `delta_ms` more milliseconds.
pub @tier(embedded)
fn timer_advance(t: RosTimer, delta_ms: Int) -> RosTimer []
    todo("ros2-nv.rosexec.timer_advance")

// Whether the timer owes a firing.
pub @tier(embedded)
fn timer_is_ready(t: RosTimer) -> Bool []
    todo("ros2-nv.rosexec.timer_is_ready")

// The timer with one owed firing taken.
pub @tier(embedded)
fn timer_fired(t: RosTimer) -> RosTimer []
    todo("ros2-nv.rosexec.timer_fired")

// How many firings the timer owes.
//
// More than one means the spin was late.  A number rather than a
// boolean, because a program that wants to know it was late can only
// find out here.
pub @tier(embedded)
fn timer_owed(t: RosTimer) -> Int []
    todo("ros2-nv.rosexec.timer_owed")

// How long until the timer next fires, in milliseconds, or 0.
//
// The number a spin passes to the transport as its wait: waiting longer
// than this makes the timer late for no reason.
pub @tier(embedded)
fn timer_next_ms(t: RosTimer) -> Int []
    todo("ros2-nv.rosexec.timer_next_ms")

// The timer, stopped.
pub @tier(embedded)
fn timer_cancel(t: RosTimer) -> RosTimer []
    todo("ros2-nv.rosexec.timer_cancel")

// The timer, started again from zero.
pub @tier(embedded)
fn timer_reset(t: RosTimer) -> RosTimer []
    todo("ros2-nv.rosexec.timer_reset")

// The shortest wait an executor should take, given its own timeout and
// a timer's, in milliseconds.
//
// A spin that waits its full timeout when a timer is due in five
// milliseconds makes that timer ninety-five milliseconds late, every
// cycle, and nothing reports it.
pub @tier(embedded)
fn spin_wait_ms(e: RosExecutor, t: RosTimer) -> Int []
    todo("ros2-nv.rosexec.spin_wait_ms")
